// XFAIL: RT-377: (RT-291's other flags) the Debug of a char that is written as `\u{..}` takes the formatter's `#`, `+` and `0` flags into the escape: `{:#?}` of '\u{7f}' is `'\u{0x7f}'` (also inside a pretty-printed array or Option), `{:+?}` is `'\u{+7f}'`, `{:08?}` is `'\u{0000007f}'` (0.30.2)
// rustc prints the EXPECT lines (twin .rs.txt): the escape is always
// `\u{7f}`. v0.28.0 to v0.30.1 the same. A str's Debug is right
// (`"a\u{7f}b"` under `{:#?}` and `{:8?}`). RT-291 is the width and
// alignment form (`{:5?}`, `{:<4?}`: padding inside the braces).
// EXPECT: '\u{7f}'
// EXPECT: [
// EXPECT:     '\u{1}',
// EXPECT:     'x',
// EXPECT: ]
// EXPECT: '\u{7f}'
// EXPECT: '\u{7f}'
fn main() {
    println("{:#?}", '\u{7f}');
    println("{:#?}", ['\u{1}', 'x']);
    println("{:+?}", '\u{7f}');
    println("{:08?}", '\u{7f}');
}
